[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 2, "caption": "Figure 1: Training dynamics define distinct generalization regimes in biological reasoning models. We compare backbone choice, continued pre-training (CPT), supervised fine-tuning (SFT), and reinforcement learning (RL) across genomics, transcriptomics, and protein tasks, and evaluate each stage on biologically meaningful in-domain (ID) and out-of-domain (OOD) splits.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic overview of a biological foundation model training and evaluation pipeline.\n\nVisible panels and labels:\n- **Backbones**: lists general-purpose LLM backbones `Qwen3-1.7B`, `Qwen3-4B`, and `Gemma4`; lists biological foundation models for `DNA: Evo 2`, `RNA: TranscriptFormer`, and `Proteins: ESM3`.\n- **Training Stages**: three sequential stages connected by arrows:\n  - `Continued pre-training (CPT)`: domain-adaptive pre-training on biological corpora.\n  - `Supervised fine-tuning (SFT)`: task-specific instruction tuning with curated datasets.\n  - `Reinforcement learning (RL)`: optimize reasoning and final answers with task-aware rewards.\n- **Biological Modalities**: three vertical modality sections:\n  - `DNA`: example token/string `ATCG`; task `Pathway prediction`; source object appears as genomic alteration nodes/network; output described as predicting pathway activation from genomic alterations.\n  - `RNA`: example token/string `AUGC`; task `Drug target identification`; source object includes a `GENE` label pointing to a target symbol; output described as identifying genes as drug targets from transcriptomic profiles.\n  - `Proteins`: example amino-acid token sequence shown as boxed letters; task `Function prediction`; output label `Function`; described as predicting protein function from amino acid sequence.\n- **Evaluation**: compares:\n  - `In-domain (ID)`: same biological pathway, same diseases, same species.\n  - `Out-of-domain (OOD)`: unseen pathways, unseen diseases, unseen species.\n  - `Findings`: SFT improves in-domain reasoning; RL enhances out-of-domain performance; trade-offs across stages.\n\nModel/interface flow:\n- Backbone models feed into training stages.\n- Training stages feed into modality-specific biological tasks.\n- Biological modality outputs are evaluated under ID and OOD settings.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- DNA nucleotide sequence example.\n- RNA nucleotide sequence example.\n- Protein amino-acid sequence example.\n- Genomic alteration/network representation.\n- Transcriptomic gene/drug-target representation.\n- Protein sequence-to-function representation."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 5, "caption": "Figure 2: Supervised fine-tuning improves in-domain performance but reduces out-of-domain robustness. As SFT compute increases, ID performance continues to improve, while OOD performance peaks early and declines, indicating over-specialization to the training data. DNA/ RNA mean and std. over 3 random seeds (we only use one seed for Proteins, due to the size of the dataset).", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThree side-by-side line-plot panels compare model performance over SFT epochs.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Left panel: `DNA`\n  - Y-axis: `Accuracy`\n  - X-axis: `SFT epochs`\n  - Epoch ticks: 0, 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32\n- Middle panel: `RNA`\n  - Y-axis: `Accuracy`\n  - X-axis: `SFT epochs`\n- Right panel: `Proteins`\n  - Y-axis: `F_max`\n  - X-axis: `SFT epochs`\n\nLegend/model interfaces:\n- `Qwen3-1.7B ID-Test`\n- `Qwen3-1.7B OOD-Test`\n- `Qwen3-4B ID-Test`\n- `Qwen3-4B OOD-Test`\n\nBiological source objects:\n- DNA sequence-related tasks\n- RNA sequence-related tasks\n- Protein sequence-related tasks\n\nTransformations/experimental variable:\n- Performance is plotted as a function of supervised fine-tuning duration, measured in `SFT epochs`.\n\nFindings visible in the figure:\n- For DNA and RNA, accuracy rises sharply from 0 to early epochs, then plateaus.\n- The 4B model generally reaches higher ID-Test performance than the 1.7B model.\n- OOD-Test curves are consistently lower than ID-Test curves.\n- Protein `F_max` improves up to roughly 4-8 epochs, then declines by 16-32 epochs, especially for OOD-Test curves."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 6, "caption": "Figure 3: Increasing data improves generalization more reliably than increasing SFT epochs. Scaling dataset size yields gains in both ID and OOD performance, but with diminishing returns, in contrast to the overfitting behavior observed when scaling epochs.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image contains two side-by-side line-plot panels comparing model performance as training dataset size increases.\n\nVisible panels and labels:\n- Left panel: titled `ID-test`\n- Right panel: titled `OOD-test`\n- Shared x-axis label: `Training dataset size (K)`\n- Y-axis label: `F_max`\n- X-axis tick values appear at approximately 0, 5, 10, 15, 20\n- Y-axis ranges roughly from 0.06 to 0.30\n- Legend in the left panel:\n  - Blue line with square markers: `Qwen3-1.7B`\n  - Orange line with circular markers: `Qwen3-4B`\n\nBiological source objects:\n- No biological source objects are visible in the image.\n\nTransformations or model interfaces:\n- The figure compares two machine learning models, `Qwen3-1.7B` and `Qwen3-4B`, across increasing training dataset sizes.\n- No model architecture diagram, interface, or data transformation pipeline is shown.\n\nFindings visible from the plots:\n- In both `ID-test` and `OOD-test`, `Qwen3-4B` generally achieves higher `F_max` than `Qwen3-1.7B`.\n- Performance generally increases with larger training dataset size.\n- On `OOD-test`, `Qwen3-4B` reaches around 0.31 `F_max` at larger dataset sizes, while `Qwen3-1.7B` remains around 0.20-0.22.\n- On `ID-test`, both models improve with dataset size, with `Qwen3-4B` peaking around 0.27-0.28 and `Qwen3-1.7B` around 0.25-0.26."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 6, "caption": "Figure 4: Reinforcement learning consistently improves out-of-domain robustness. Starting from strong SFT checkpoints, RL increases both ID and OOD performance, with the largest gains in OOD and diminishing returns after the first few epochs. DNA/ RNA mean and std. over 3 random seeds (we only use one seed for Proteins, due to the size of the dataset).", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific plot with three side-by-side line charts labeled **DNA**, **RNA**, and **Proteins**.\n\nVisible structure:\n- **Panel 1: DNA**\n  - Y-axis: **Accuracy**\n  - X-axis: **RL epochs**\n  - Epoch values shown: 0, 1, 2, 4, 8, 16\n  - Four curves compare:\n    - Qwen3-1.7B ID-Test\n    - Qwen3-1.7B OOD-Test\n    - Qwen3-4B ID-Test\n    - Qwen3-4B OOD-Test\n  - Solid lines appear to represent ID-Test; dashed lines appear to represent OOD-Test.\n  - Shaded bands indicate variability or uncertainty around some curves.\n  - Accuracy generally improves with RL epochs, especially for the Qwen3-4B ID-Test curve, which reaches the highest DNA accuracy.\n\n- **Panel 2: RNA**\n  - Y-axis: **Accuracy**\n  - X-axis: **RL epochs**\n  - Epoch values shown: 0, 1, 2, 4, 8, 16\n  - Same four model/test-condition curves as the DNA panel.\n  - Accuracy drops sharply at epoch 1 for some curves, then recovers over later RL epochs.\n  - Qwen3-4B ID-Test maintains the highest RNA accuracy by later epochs.\n\n- **Panel 3: Proteins**\n  - Y-axis: **Fmax**\n  - X-axis: **RL epochs**\n  - Epoch values shown: 0, 1, 2, 4, 8\n  - Multiple curves compare model/test conditions, visually consistent with the DNA/RNA legend.\n  - Fmax rises strongly from epoch 0 to later epochs, peaking around epoch 4 for the top orange curve, then slightly decreasing or plateauing by epoch 8.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- DNA sequences\n- RNA sequences\n- Proteins\n\nModel/interface context visible:\n- Models compared: **Qwen3-1.7B** and **Qwen3-4B**\n- Evaluation settings: **ID-Test** and **OOD-Test**\n- Training/intervention variable: **RL epochs**\n\nMain visible finding:\n- Reinforcement learning epochs are associated with improved performance across DNA, RNA, and protein tasks, with the larger **Qwen3-4B** model generally outperforming **Qwen3-1.7B**, especially on ID-Test curves."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 7, "caption": "Figure 5: Continued pre-training improves the effectiveness of downstream post-training. CPT improves both SFT and RL performance, with the largest gains appearing after RL and in out-of-domain settings.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel bar chart comparing model accuracy across task settings for DNA and RNA.\n\nVisible structure:\n- Four panels arranged horizontally.\n- Left two panels are grouped under the header “DNA”:\n  - “ID-test”\n  - “OOD-test”\n- Right two panels are grouped under the header “RNA”:\n  - “ID-test”\n  - “OOD-test”\n- Y-axis label: “Accuracy”, ranging approximately from 0.0 to 1.0.\n- X-axis categories in each panel:\n  - Base\n  - SFT\n  - RL\n  - CPT+SFT\n  - CPT+RL\n- Legend:\n  - Blue bars: Qwen3-1.7B\n  - Orange bars: Qwen3-4B\n\nBiological source objects:\n- DNA sequence tasks\n- RNA sequence tasks\n\nModel interfaces / transformations shown:\n- Comparison of baseline and training/adaptation strategies:\n  - Base\n  - supervised fine-tuning, SFT\n  - reinforcement learning, RL\n  - continued pretraining plus SFT, CPT+SFT\n  - continued pretraining plus RL, CPT+RL\n- Comparison across two model sizes: Qwen3-1.7B and Qwen3-4B.\n- Evaluation split comparison: in-distribution test and out-of-distribution test.\n\nVisible findings:\n- For DNA, RL and CPT+RL achieve the highest accuracies, especially on ID-test and OOD-test.\n- For RNA, Base accuracy is low, while SFT, RL, CPT+SFT, and CPT+RL substantially improve performance.\n- CPT+RL appears among the best-performing settings across DNA and RNA panels.\n- Qwen3-4B is generally similar to or slightly higher than Qwen3-1.7B in many conditions."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 7, "caption": "Figure 6: Stronger backbones improve performance achievable with post-training but preserve training dynamics. G-R does not display an initial drop in performance when starting RL, unlike Q1-R and generally performs better OOD. Mean and std. over 3 random seeds.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image contains two side-by-side line-plot panels, both titled “RNA,” showing model accuracy across training epochs.\n\nLeft panel:\n- X-axis: “SFT epochs” with ticks at 0, 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32.\n- Y-axis: “Accuracy,” ranging roughly from 0.2 to 1.0.\n- Multiple colored line series compare methods/models, including Qwen3-1.7B ID-Test, Qwen3-1.7B OOD-Test, Gemma4-E2B ID-Test, Gemma4-E2B OOD-Test, Qwen3-4B ID-Test, and Qwen3-4B OOD-Test.\n- Accuracy generally increases sharply after early SFT epochs, with ID-test curves reaching higher values than OOD-test curves.\n\nRight panel:\n- X-axis: “RL epochs” with ticks at 0, 1, 2, 4, 8, 16.\n- Y-axis: “Accuracy.”\n- The same types of model/test-condition curves are shown.\n- Accuracy varies after RL training; several ID-test curves remain high or improve, while OOD-test curves are lower and flatter.\n\nBiological source object:\n- The figure concerns RNA, as indicated by the panel titles, but no RNA molecules, sequences, structures, cells, tissues, or biological images are directly shown.\n\nTransformations/model interface:\n- The figure compares machine learning model performance across supervised fine-tuning epochs and reinforcement learning epochs.\n- It reports accuracy for in-distribution and out-of-distribution test sets across different language-model variants.\n\nVisible finding:\n- SFT appears to substantially improve RNA-task accuracy from epoch 0 to later epochs.\n- RL results show model- and test-condition-dependent changes, with ID-test accuracy generally exceeding OOD-test accuracy."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 8, "caption": "Figure 7: Optimal adaptation requires asymmetric capacity across training stages. Higher LoRA rank benefits SFT, while lower rank is sufficient for RL, indicating that different stages require different adaptation capacity (both for ID and OOD tasks). Shown are results for drug target identification (RNA) tasks.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image shows four heatmap panels comparing model performance across LoRA rank settings.\n\nVisible panels:\n- `Qwen3-1.7B — ID-test`\n- `Qwen3-1.7B — OOD-test`\n- `Qwen3-4B — ID-test`\n- `Qwen3-4B — OOD-test`\n\nAxes and labels:\n- X-axis: `RL LoRA rank`, with values `4`, `16`, `64`\n- Y-axis: `SFT LoRA rank`, with values `256`, `64`, `16`\n- Each heatmap cell contains a numeric score.\n\nContent:\n- The two Qwen3-1.7B panels use a blue color scale.\n- The two Qwen3-4B panels use an orange color scale.\n- Scores are highest around RL LoRA rank `16` and SFT LoRA rank `256` or `64`.\n- Qwen3-4B has higher displayed scores than Qwen3-1.7B across both ID-test and OOD-test.\n- OOD-test scores are generally lower than ID-test scores for both model sizes.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy, molecular structures, anatomical imagery, or biological transformations are visible. The figure is a machine learning evaluation heatmap comparing model configurations and test settings."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 9, "caption": "Figure 8: Under a fixed post-training budget, a small amount of SFT followed by more RL gives the best ID-OOD trade-off. Across DNA and RNA, 1-3 SFT epochs followed by larger RL budgets generally give the strongest OOD accuracy, while larger SFT allocations achieve better ID performance.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a two-panel line plot comparing model accuracy across training epochs.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Left panel titled “DNA”\n- Right panel titled “RNA”\n\nAxes and labels:\n- Y-axis: “Accuracy”, ranging roughly from 0.4 to 1.0\n- X-axis: “SFT / RL epochs”\n- X-axis tick labels appear as epoch pairs such as `0/8`, `1/7`, `2/6`, `3/5`, `4/4`, `5/3`, `6/2`, `7/1`, `8/0`\n\nLegend/model interfaces:\n- Blue solid line: `Qwen3-1.7B`\n- Blue dashed line: `Qwen3-1.7B OOD`\n- Green solid line: `Gemma4-2B`\n- Green dashed line: `Gemma4-2B OOD`\n\nBiological source objects:\n- DNA sequences/tasks in the left panel\n- RNA sequences/tasks in the right panel\n\nFindings visible in the plot:\n- DNA accuracies are generally high for in-distribution curves, around 0.85-0.95, with OOD curves lower around 0.70-0.80.\n- RNA accuracies are more variable than DNA.\n- In RNA, the green solid `Gemma4-2B` curve peaks near mid-epoch settings and then declines, while the blue solid `Qwen3-1.7B` curve rises sharply at `8/0`.\n- OOD performance is consistently lower than in-distribution performance in both DNA and RNA panels."]}, {"figure_index": 9, "page_no": 9, "caption": "Figure 9: RL shifts the ID-OOD frontier across modalities. Each point is a trained checkpoint; color denotes training stage and marker shape denotes backbone. RL generally improves OOD performance at comparable ID performance across DNA, RNA, and protein tasks.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a three-panel scientific plot comparing model performance under in-distribution and out-of-distribution evaluation.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Left: `DNA (Pathway Prediction)`\n  - X-axis: `ID Accuracy`\n  - Y-axis: `OOD Accuracy`\n  - Scatter points for multiple models/conditions.\n- Middle: `RNA (Drug Target ID)`\n  - X-axis: `ID Accuracy`\n  - Y-axis: `OOD Accuracy`\n  - Scatter points comparing ID and OOD accuracy.\n- Right: `Proteins (Function Prediction)`\n  - X-axis: `ID Fmax`\n  - Y-axis: `OOD Fmax`\n  - Scatter points comparing ID and OOD Fmax.\n\nLegend/model interfaces:\n- `SFT-only`\n- `SFT+RL`\n- `Qwen3-1.7B`\n- `Qwen3-4B`\n- `Gemma4-E2B`\n\nBiological source objects:\n- DNA sequences/tasks for pathway prediction.\n- RNA-related task labeled drug target identification.\n- Protein function prediction.\n\nTransformations/visual encodings:\n- Scatter plots compare ID versus OOD performance.\n- A diagonal dashed line marks equal ID and OOD performance.\n- Pink shaded triangular regions indicate performance below the diagonal, where OOD performance is lower than ID performance.\n- Different marker colors/shapes distinguish training method and base model.\n\nVisible findings:\n- Across DNA, RNA, and protein panels, most points lie below the diagonal, indicating OOD performance is generally worse than ID performance.\n- `SFT+RL` points generally appear higher than `SFT-only` points, especially in DNA and protein panels.\n- Protein function prediction shows the strongest upper-right cluster for some `SFT+RL` model points, reaching high ID and OOD Fmax values."]}, {"figure_index": 10, "page_no": 29, "caption": "Figure 10: Optimal adaptation requires asymmetric capacity across training stages. Higher LoRA rank benefits SFT, while lower rank is sufficient for RL, indicating that different stages require different adaptation capacity (both for ID and OOD tasks). Shown are results for pathway prediction (DNA) tasks.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image shows four adjacent heatmap panels comparing percentage values across LoRA rank settings.\n\nVisible panels:\n- `Qwen3-1.7B — ID`\n- `Qwen3-1.7B — OOD`\n- `Qwen3-4B — ID`\n- `Qwen3-4B — OOD`\n\nAxes and labels:\n- X-axis: `RL LoRA rank`, with columns `4`, `16`, `64`\n- Y-axis: `SFT LoRA rank`, with rows `256`, `64`, `16`\n- Each cell contains a percentage value.\n- Color encodes magnitude, using blue heatmaps for Qwen3-1.7B panels and orange/red heatmaps for Qwen3-4B panels.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- None visible.\n\nTransformations or model interfaces:\n- The figure compares model performance across combinations of SFT LoRA rank and RL LoRA rank.\n- It includes two model sizes, `Qwen3-1.7B` and `Qwen3-4B`, evaluated under `ID` and `OOD` conditions.\n\nFindings visible from the figure:\n- `Qwen3